    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lisinopril actually works? like specifically with the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (raas)? i'm trying to wrap my head around the step by step process for my exams.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril is an ACE inhibitor, which specifically targets the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS). It blocks the enzyme Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) from converting Angiotensin I to the potent vasoconstrictor, Angiotensin II. By reducing Angiotensin II, lisinopril causes blood vessels to relax and widen (vasodilation), effectively lowering blood pressure. This decrease also reduces aldosterone secretion, leading to less sodium and water retention.",
      "source": "target",
