    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thx. can u go a bit deeper into the enzyme part? like, how does the lisinopril interaction actually happen at the molecular level to stop the conversion? i need to know the specific mechanism of how it binds to the ACE enzyme.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril acts as a competitive inhibitor at the active site of the ACE enzyme. Its structure mimics the natural substrate, Angiotensin I, allowing it to fit precisely into the enzyme's binding pocket. Once bound, lisinopril physically occupies the active site, preventing Angiotensin I from binding and undergoing the necessary peptidyl cleavage to form Angiotensin II. This strong inhibition effectively deactivates the enzyme's catalytic function.",
      "source": "target",
