    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so since it's a competitive inhibitor, does the concentration of lisinopril in the bloodstream change how effectively it blocks the enzyme? like what happens if the drug levels drop or if there's a lot of angiotensin i present?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, because lisinopril is a competitive inhibitor, its effectiveness is directly related to its concentration relative to Angiotensin I. If the concentration of lisinopril increases, it outcompetes Angiotensin I for the active site, enhancing the blockade. Conversely, if Angiotensin I levels rise significantly, you would theoretically need a proportionally higher concentration of lisinopril to maintain the same level of enzyme inhibition.",
      "source": "target",
